# Solving Linear Systems on Quantum Hardware with Hybrid HHL++

**arXiv:** 2110.15958
**Authors:** Romina Yalovetzky, Pierre Minssen, Dylan Herman, Marco Pistoia
(JPMorganChase, Global Technology Applied Research)
**Venue:** Scientific Reports, v6 (10 Jul 2024)
**Local PDF:** work/paper.pdf (2.1 MB); text extraction work/paper.txt (pdftotext)

## Abstract (paraphrase for index; see paper.txt for verbatim)
Extends the Hybrid HHL algorithm for solving Ax=b on NISQ hardware by
(i) a novel classical procedure for choosing the QPE scaling factor gamma, and
(ii) semiclassical QPE (mid-circuit measurement + classical feedback) to reduce
qubit count and two-qubit-gate cost. Demonstrates the largest-to-date HHL
execution on Quantinuum H-series trapped-ion hardware for portfolio-optimization
instances derived from S&P 500 assets, at two-qubit gate depth up to 291.

## Key quantitative anchors (from paper Table 1 / Table 2)
- QPE ZZPhase gates: standard 63/88/115 (3/4/5-bit) vs semiclassical 57/76/95.
- QPE qubits: standard 5/6/7 vs semiclassical flat 3/3/3.
- Hardware semiclassical-QPE fidelity: 98.6% (3-bit), 90.4% (4-bit), 42.6% (5-bit).

## Replication scope note
This QC-100 replication reproduced the algorithm-level resource-reduction +
fidelity headline (C1-C4) on a 2x2 kappa=2 instance via Qiskit Aer statevector.
Novel gamma-selection (C5) and Quantinuum hardware experiments (C6/C7) not
reproduced (hardware-access + full-artifact scope). See report/REPORT.md and
report/failure_analysis.md.
